body:
Found in a bug sweep. Verified by reading the source directly.

## Summary

Shop creation validates `price` but never validates `priceCombo`. A player creating a **combo** shop can type a negative sell-side price, and it is accepted and persisted. The sign then renders a negative price, and the sell path pays the buyer rather than charging them.

## The gap

`getShopPricePair` parses both fields, then validates only the first:

```java
// core/src/main/java/com/snowgears/shop/util/ShopCreationUtil.java:509-556
String[] multiplePrices = line3.split(" ");
if (multiplePrices.length > 1) {
    if (multiplePrices[0].contains("."))
        price = Double.parseDouble(multiplePrices[0]);
    else
        price = Long.parseLong(multiplePrices[0]);
    if (multiplePrices[1].contains("."))
        priceCombo = Double.parseDouble(multiplePrices[1]);
    else
        priceCombo = Long.parseLong(multiplePrices[1]);
}
...
//only allow price to be zero if the type is selling
if (price < 0 || (price == 0 && shopType == ShopType.BARTER)) {
    ShopMessage.sendMessage("interaction_issue", "line3", player, null);
    return null;
}
return new PricePair(price, priceCombo);
```

`priceCombo` passes through untouched. `Long.parseLong` accepts a leading `-`, and `Double.parseDouble` likewise.

`getShopPriceCombo` has the same shape: it returns `priceCombo` after only catching `NumberFormatException`, with no range check.

## Where it lands

```java
// ShopCreationUtil.java:154
final AbstractShop shop = AbstractShop.create(signBlock.getLocation(), player.getUniqueId(),
        pricePair.getPrice(), pricePair.getPriceCombo(), amount, isAdmin, type, signDirection);
```

`priceCombo` becomes `ComboShop.priceSell`:

```java
// core/src/main/java/com/snowgears/shop/shop/ComboShop.java:20-30
public ComboShop(Location signLoc, UUID player, double pri, double priSell, int amt, Boolean admin, BlockFace facing) {
    ...
    this.priceSell = priSell;
    ...
    return Shop.getPlugin().getPriceString(this.priceSell, false);
```

and it is rendered on the sign through `getPriceComboString`.

## Failure scenario

A player creates a combo shop with `100 -50`:

- buy price `100` — validated, accepted;
- sell price `-50` — never validated.

The sign displays a negative price for the sell side. A buyer purchasing from that shop is *paid* 50 rather than charged, draining the seller's stock into the buyer's inventory plus a gain.

This needs combo creation to be enabled and the player to have the create permission for that shop type, so it is a self-inflicted-or-griefing bug rather than a permission bypass — but on a server where players may create shops freely, any player can mint currency from thin air at a shop they own.

## Why the sweep's finding understated it

The claim framed this as "accepts negative and zero prices verbatim", which reads as cosmetic. The consequence is the part worth acting on: it is a **currency-generation** vector, because the payout path uses the same stored `priceSell`.

## Suggested fix

Validate both fields at the point of parsing, and only for shop types that use the combo price:

```java
if (price < 0 || (price == 0 && shopType == ShopType.BARTER)) {
    ShopMessage.sendMessage("interaction_issue", "line3", player, null);
    return null;
}
if (priceCombo < 0) {
    ShopMessage.sendMessage("interaction_issue", "line3", player, null);
    return null;
}
```

A defence-in-depth check is worth adding at load time too: a shop whose persisted `priceSell` is negative was either created before this fix or hand-edited, and `AbstractShop.load` should reject or clamp it rather than trusting the file.

## Note

Found during a 12-dimension bug sweep with adversarial verification. I have not created such a shop on a running server. The trace is from reading `getShopPricePair`, `getShopPriceCombo`, and `ComboShop`'s constructor. That `priceCombo` is unvalidated is unambiguous from the source; whether the payout path uses `priceSell` as-is is worth confirming before assigning a final severity, since that determines whether this mints currency or merely displays a wrong sign.
